## 4. Most likely root cause (needs dev confirmation against live templates)

Given the pattern above, this is almost certainly one or a combination of:

1. **Empty/near-empty result sets.** These are filter pages (qualification × city, or district × category). Many city/qualification/district combinations currently have zero or very few live job postings. If the template just renders a "No jobs found" message (or an empty list) with little else, Google sees a thin/empty page and marks it Soft 404 — even though the server returns HTTP 200.
2. **Duplicate/boilerplate templates.** If the page shell (header, filters, footer) is nearly identical across all city/district variants and the only thing that changes is the URL and a heading, Google can also flag it as Soft 404 for lacking unique, substantive content.
3. **Pages published to the sitemap before content existed.** The step-changes in the trend (3→13→67→80 pages within days) strongly suggest a bulk URL rollout (new city/qualification combinations added to the sitemap) that outpaced actual job-posting coverage for those combinations.

