### 5.1 Not found (404) — 607 pages

This is nearly 7× the Soft 404 count and deserves its own quick audit before assuming it's fine:

- If these are pages that were **intentionally removed** (e.g., old job postings that expired), returning a real 404 is correct — no action needed, just make sure they're **not still linked internally** or listed in the sitemap.
- If any of these are **broken internal links** (typos in generated URLs, renamed slugs without redirects), those need a 301 redirect to the correct URL.
- Quick check: cross-reference this list against `sitemap.xml` — a URL that's both in the sitemap **and** 404ing is a bug, not an intentional removal.

### 5.2 Excluded by 'noindex' tag — 60 pages, validation "Not Started"

Confirm these 60 pages are **meant** to be noindexed (e.g., filter/pagination variants, thank-you pages, admin/preview routes). If any of them are legitimate content pages that got an accidental `noindex` tag from a template bug, that's a fast, high-value fix — pull the list and eyeball it before ignoring it.

### 5.3 Site-wide health signals worth noting

From the Search Console **Overview** page (same account, same date range):

- **Search clicks: 3 total** over the reporting window — consistent with the indexing problem: with only 672 of 2,474 known pages indexed, and many of the indexed pages being thin, organic traffic has almost no surface area to be found on.
- **HTTPS enhancement: 19 valid, 0 non-HTTPS** — fine, no action needed.
- **Breadcrumbs enhancement: 18 valid, 0 invalid** — fine, but only 18 pages have breadcrumbs at all; extending breadcrumbs to the category/location templates (Section 11) would help both users and Google understand page hierarchy.
- **Job Postings (rich result) enhancement: 0 valid, 0 invalid** — **no pages currently use `JobPosting` structured data.** This is a missed opportunity independent of the Soft 404 issue: individual job-detail pages are eligible for Google's Job Search rich results, which typically drives meaningfully more clicks than a standard blue link. Recommend adding `JobPosting` schema to actual job-detail pages (not the category/location listing pages covered by this report) as a follow-up project.

## 6. Recommended fix (developer action items)

- [ ] **Add a real no-results state.** For any `{qualification}-jobs-in-{city}`, `department/{dept}`, or `districts/{district}/{category}` page where the live result count is 0 (or below a minimum threshold, e.g. 3), either:
  - **noindex** that specific page (via meta robots tag) until it has enough content, and remove it from the sitemap, **or**
  - **301 redirect** it to the nearest relevant parent page (e.g. `/jobs-in-{state}` or `/districts/{district}`) if one exists.
- [ ] **Only submit URLs to the sitemap once they have minimum viable content** (e.g. ≥3–5 real listings), instead of generating the full city × qualification × district matrix upfront.
- [ ] **Differentiate template content** for pages that do have listings: unique intro copy, breadcrumbs, related-category links, last-updated date — so pages aren't near-duplicates of each other even when listings are thin.
- [ ] **Re-run validation in GSC** after deploying the fix (Page indexing → Soft 404 → Validate Fix). Google will re-crawl the 82 URLs below; expect the count to drop only after Google re-crawls (can take 1–4 weeks).
- [ ] Spot-check a handful of the URLs in the table below directly in a browser (view source / rendered HTML) to confirm which of the causes in Section 4 applies before writing the fix — the fix differs slightly for "genuinely zero listings" vs. "near-duplicate boilerplate."

## 7. Investigation process — how to work through the 82 URLs

Don't fix all 82 blind. Different URLs in Section 3 will need different fixes depending on whether they currently have real listings or not. Work through them with a tracking sheet:

**Step 1 — Export and build a tracking sheet** with these columns:

| URL                | Current live listing count | Word count (excl. nav/footer) | Has unique intro copy? | Has FAQ/related links? | Verdict | Fix |
| ------------------ | -------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------- | ------- | --- |
| /jobs-in-telangana | ?                          | ?                             | ?                      | ?                      | ?       | ?   |

**Step 2 — Visit each URL (or query the DB directly) and check:**

- Live job/listing count for that city/qualification/district/department combination
- Whether the rendered HTML has substantive unique text, or is just a header + "No results" + footer
- Whether the page is in `sitemap.xml`
- Whether the page has a self-referencing canonical tag

**Step 3 — Sort every URL into one of three buckets**, then apply the matching fix from Section 8.

## 8. Decision matrix — which fix applies to which page

Not every Soft 404 page should be treated the same way. Use this matrix once Section 7's audit is done:

| Situation                                                                                                                                 | Recommended HTTP status                           | Action                                                                                                                        |
| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Page has real listings now, just thin copy                                                                                                | 200 (keep)                                        | Enrich content per Section 9 — do not noindex or remove                                                                       |
| Page currently has 0 listings, but the city/state/department is a real, ongoing category (e.g. a state page)                              | 200 (keep)                                        | Add substantial evergreen content + "related jobs" fallback per Section 9, so the page has value even with 0 current listings |
| Page currently has 0 listings for a narrow/rare combination (e.g. a small district × niche category) that may never get meaningful volume | 404, 410, or `noindex` + remove from sitemap      | Don't keep publishing near-empty long-tail combinations just to grow page count                                               |
| Page is obsolete / superseded by a renamed URL                                                                                            | 301 redirect                                      | Redirect to the new URL, don't leave the old one live                                                                         |
| Page was auto-generated by a city/qualification/district matrix with no real underlying data                                              | `noindex` + remove from sitemap until data exists | Regenerate later once listings exist                                                                                          |

**Do not delete existing working templates.** The matrix above says "remove weak long-tail combinations," not "shut down the location/category page system" — pages like `/jobs-in-telangana` (a real state) should stay and be strengthened; it's specifically the thin, low-value, rarely-searched combinations that should be pruned or noindexed until they have content.

## 9. Content requirements for pages you keep at 200

For any page you decide to keep live (per Section 8), it needs to clear a real content bar — not just exist. Minimum target per page:

- **Unique introductory copy** (not copy-pasted across every city/state variant) — roughly 300–500+ words covering: what the page is about, the region/department/qualification context, and why it's useful
- **Live listings**, if any exist for that combination
- **A genuine "no current openings" fallback** when listings are 0 — _never_ a bare "No Jobs Found" with nothing else. Fallback should surface:
  - Related jobs (same qualification, nearby city/state)
  - Popular departments recruiting in that state/qualification
  - Links to broader parent category (e.g. state page from a city page)
- **FAQs** relevant to that qualification/department/region (eligibility, selection process, application process)
- **Internal links** to and from: homepage, parent category, sibling categories, related job postings
- **Breadcrumb navigation** reflecting the page's place in the hierarchy (only 18 pages sitewide currently have breadcrumbs — see Section 5.3)
- **Self-referencing canonical tag**
- **Last-updated date**, so the page doesn't look static/abandoned to Google or users

## 10. Technical / implementation checklist

Framework-agnostic checklist for the dev doing the fix. (If the site is built on Next.js — flag to confirm — the specific pattern is to check for empty data server-side and call `notFound()` rather than rendering an empty 200 page; the same principle applies in any stack.)

- [ ] Server-side: when a dynamic route resolves to 0 listings **and** the category isn't a "keep" per Section 8, return a real `404`/`410` status — never render an empty page with `200`.
- [ ] Server-side: when a dynamic route resolves to an **invalid** slug (city/district/department that doesn't exist in the DB), return `404` — don't silently render a blank template.
- [ ] Confirm every "keep" page has a self-canonical tag pointing to itself, not to a parent category.
- [ ] Confirm `sitemap.xml` only lists URLs that are meant to be indexed — pull any page marked for `noindex`/`404`/`410` out of the sitemap immediately (this matters even before content fixes ship, since it stops Google recrawling and re-flagging pages you've already decided to retire).
- [ ] Confirm `robots.txt` isn't accidentally blocking crawl of pages you actually want indexed.
- [ ] Add/verify meta title + meta description are unique per page (not templated identically across every city/state).

## 11. Structured data (schema) — where it applies

- **Category/location listing pages** (the ones in this report): `BreadcrumbList`, `CollectionPage` or `WebPage`, and `FAQPage` (if the page has an FAQ block) are appropriate.
- **Individual job-detail pages** (not covered by this Soft 404 report, but flagged in Section 5.3): add `JobPosting` schema. Site currently has 0 pages using this enhancement — it's a real opportunity for Google Job Search visibility, separate from the indexing fix above.
- Don't add `JobPosting` schema to the aggregate/category pages — it belongs on the individual job listing only.

## 12. Testing & validation

1. Spot-check a sample of "keep" and "remove/noindex" pages after the fix ships using Search Console's **URL Inspection tool** (live test, not just cached).
2. Run the **Rich Results Test** on any page with new `FAQPage`/`BreadcrumbList`/`JobPosting` markup.
3. Run a crawl (Screaming Frog / Sitebulb, or an internal script) across all 82 URLs to confirm HTTP status codes match what Section 8 intended (200 for "keep," 404/410 for "retire," 301 for "redirect").
4. In Search Console: **Page indexing → Soft 404 → Validate Fix.** Google will re-crawl the 82 URLs in Section 13 below on its own schedule — this typically takes 1–4 weeks, not immediate.
5. Monitor the Overview page (Indexing graph, Impressions/Clicks) weekly after validation starts, not just the Soft 404 count in isolation — the real success signal is the "Indexed" line moving up and "Not indexed" moving down (Section 2), plus the click count in Section 5.3 starting to move off of 3.

